<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Roads and Kingdoms (al-Bakri)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1068</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Roads and Kingdoms (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitob">Kitab</a> al-Masalik wa'l-Mamalik) is a geographical work completed in 1068 by the Andalusi scholar Abu Ubayd al-Bakri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Compiled from earlier writings and travellers' reports rather than his own journeys, it describes the routes and lands of the known world. Its account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, including the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and its capital, is among the most important sources for the region's early history.</p></div>
